.TH EQN2HOC 2
.SH NAME
eqn2hoc: translate \- translate eqn(7) math notation into hoc(2) expression source
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "eqn2hoc.m";
eqn2hoc := load Eqn2hoc Eqn2hoc->PATH;

(hocsrc, err) := eqn2hoc->translate("x sup 2 + y sup 2");
if(err != nil)
	...
e := hoc->compile(hocsrc);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Eqn2hoc
translates
.IR eqn (7)
math notation \(em the troff equation-formatter syntax
.RB ( "x sup 2" ", " "a over b" ", " "sqrt x" ,
Greek letter names, and so on) \(em into
.IR hoc (2)
expression source, ready for
.BR hoc->compile() .
.I Translate
takes one plain expression string (the text that would sit between a
real document's
.B .EQ
and
.B .EN
lines, or inside
.BR $$ ... $$ )
and returns
.RI ( hocsrc ,
.BR nil )
on success, or
.RI ( nil ,
an error naming exactly which construct isn't supported and why).
.PP
Real
.I eqn
(Kernighan and Cherry's formatter; the real source, plan9port's
.BR src/cmd/eqn ,
~2500 lines across 26 files, was fetched and read in full to build
this) has
.B no
computational semantics anywhere in it \(em every one of its "boxes" is
troff drawing code: widths, baselines, font-switch escapes, for
.I typesetting
an expression on a page, never for evaluating one. Its own keyword
table maps
.RB ( sin ", " cos ", " log ,
and so on) to a roman-font escape string, not a function call \(em real
.I eqn
never treats them as callable. So this is
.B not
a 1:1 translation of
.IR eqn 's
behavior \(em there is none to preserve \(em it is a new semantic
mapping of
.IR eqn 's
real, familiar
.I syntax
(its grammar shape, its keyword vocabulary, both followed faithfully)
onto
.IR hoc 's
scalar expression language, which
.IR eqn 's
own source never had.
.SS "What maps, and why"
.TP
.B sup
Hoc's
.BR ^ ,
real exponentiation.
.I Eqn
itself only ever draws a raised box \(em but
.RI \*(lq x " sup 2" \*(rq
unambiguously means x squared to every reader, so this is the one
construct where the typeset appearance and a natural hoc meaning
coincide exactly.
.TP
.B over
Hoc's
.BR / ,
division, by the same reasoning.
.TP
.B sqrt
Hoc's own
.B sqrt()
builtin.
.TP
.B "{ } and left/right"
Grouping.
.IR Eqn 's
.B only
real grouping construct is
.BR "{ }" ;
bare
.RB "" "( )"
typed with no
.B left / right
are ordinary
.B text
characters in real
.IR eqn ,
not delimiters (confirmed directly in
.BR lex.c :
.B getstr()
does not stop scanning at
.RB "" ( " or " )).
.TP
.BR "+ - = == != > < >= <= " "and bare juxtaposition"
Ordinary hoc arithmetic, comparison, and multiplication.
Real
.IR eqn 's
own grammar has
.B none
of this \(em
.RI \*(lq eqn :
box | eqn box\*(rq
is just a sequence of boxes typeset side by side, with
.B +
itself being ordinary text, no different from any other word.
Refusing every juxtaposition as unsupported would refuse nearly every
real equation (an arithmetic operator, or "4ac"-style implicit
multiplication, is exactly this), so this is a second new-semantics
layer, on the same footing as
.BR sup / over
above.
Precedence, loosest to tightest: compare, then
.BR + / \- ,
then
.BR * / / /over /juxtaposition
(left to right, equal precedence), then
.BR sqrt /sub/sup/diacriticals.
Every operator is wrapped in explicit hoc parens on the way out, so
hoc's own precedence never has to agree with this parser's.
.TP
.BR sin ", " cos ", " tan ", " sinh ", " cosh ", " tanh ", " log ", " exp
A real hoc function call, when the word is immediately followed by a
.RB "" "{ ... }"
or
.B "left ( ... right )"
group \(em the real
.I eqn
convention for a roman-set word next to a parenthesized box. The bare
word alone is an ordinary hoc identifier otherwise, exactly as
ambiguous as it already is in real
.I eqn
(nothing there distinguishes "x" the variable from "sin" the word
either).
.TP
.B sub
Folded into one hoc identifier: "x sub i" becomes the single name
.BR x_i .
Hoc has no array or index type (the same boundary
.IR danby-tks-plan.md 's
own
.I Orbital
family hits), so this is name-mangling, not real indexing \(em refused,
with a clear error, unless both sides are already a simple bare name.
.TP
.BR hat ", " vec ", " dyad ", " dot ", " dotdot ", " bar ", " lowbar ", "
.BR highbar ", " tilde ", " utilde ", " under
Folded into an identifier the same way: "x hat" becomes
.BR xhat .
Same simple-name-only rule as
.BR sub .
.TP
.BR size / font / roman / italic / bold / fat ", " fwd / up / back / down
No-ops: parsed (to consume their own argument correctly) and
discarded, keeping the box they wrap unchanged. Pure typesetting, no
numeric meaning to preserve or drop.
.SS "What is refused, and why"
.TP
.BR sum / prod / int / integral / union / inter
No computational meaning even in real
.I eqn
\(em these are special
.B characters
drawn with sub/sup as visual limits, never evaluated. There is nothing
here to translate, not merely nowhere to put the result.
.TP
.BR matrix / pile / lpile / cpile / rpile / col / lcol / ccol / rcol / above
Need an array or matrix; hoc has none. The same boundary as
.BR sub ,
but too structural to name-mangle away.
.TP
.B mark/lineup
Troff alignment constructs, no hoc meaning.
.TP
.B from/to
Only meaningful attached to
.BR sum / int / prod ,
which have no hoc target either.
.TP
.BR define / tdefine / ndefine / ifdef / delim / gsize / gfont / include / copy / space
.IR Eqn 's
own macro/preprocessing layer. Out of scope: this library takes one
plain expression string, not a full
.BR .EQ / .EN
document with its own macro system.
.TP
.B "quoted \(dq...\(dq text"
A troff label, no hoc meaning.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/eqn2hoc.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/eqn2hoc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (2),
.IR eqn2hoctest (1),
.IR pde (2)
.SH BUGS
An
.I eqn
word that collides with one of hoc's own reserved words
(if/while/for/func/proc/return/print/read) still reaches
.BR hoc->compile() ,
which refuses it on its own \(em not specially checked here.
.IR Eqn 's
own reserved-word table happens to include "if" and "for" as
roman-typeset words, so this can occur with entirely ordinary-looking
input.
.PP
Unary minus/plus require a space before the operand
.RI ( \*(lq \- " b" \*(rq
not
.RI \*(lq \- b \*(rq )\c
, because real
.IR eqn 's
own tokenizer (faithfully reproduced here) splits only on whitespace
and a handful of special characters \(em
.RI \*(lq \- b \*(rq
with no space is one opaque token to real
.I eqn
too, not a minus sign followed by a name.
.PP
Comparison operators
.RB ( == "" != "" < "" <= "" > "" >= )
do not chain \(em
.RI \*(lq a " < b < c" \*(rq
is a syntax error (an unexpected trailing token), not
.RI "(a<b)<c\ or\ a<b\ &&\ b<c."
.PP
No test exercises a real
.BR .EQ / .EN
document end to end (delimiters, multiple equations, macros) \(em only
the inner expression syntax this library actually targets.
